## Taxonomy of Failure Modes

| Mode | Name | Severity | Primary Risk |
|------|------|----------|-------------|
| 5.1 | Declared-but-not-read | High | False grounding |
| 5.2 | Hallucinated Metadata | Medium | Trust erosion |
| 5.3 | Tool Substitution | Medium | Measurement gap |
| 5.4 | Over-Eager Loading | Low | Token waste |
| 5.5 | Under-Matching | Medium | Missing guidance |
| 5.6 | Protocol Shape Divergence | Low | Parse brittleness |
| 5.7 | Stochastic Instability | Medium | Unreliable compliance |

### 5.1 Declared-but-not-read (High)

Model lists rules in its PRE-FLIGHT header that it never accessed via any tool call. This is the highest-severity failure mode because it is **invisible without tool-trace auditing**. The header looks fully compliant; only by inspecting the tool trace can you detect the deception.

### 5.2 Hallucinated Metadata (Medium)

Model declares specific line counts in citations that don't match actual file sizes. Numbers are always in the right ballpark but systematically off by 10–30%.

### 5.3 Tool Substitution (Medium)

Model uses shell commands (`bash`) instead of `read_file` to access rule content, creating a measurement blindspot in the eval harness.

### 5.4 Over-Eager Loading (Low)

Model loads significantly more rules than required. Not a trust issue — the model is being thorough. Context window is consumed by marginally-relevant rules.

### 5.5 Under-Matching (Medium)

Model fails to discover rules that keyword or file-extension triggers should have surfaced. File-extension triggers (`file=snowflake.yml`) and abbreviated keywords are particularly prone to being missed.

### 5.6 Protocol Shape Divergence (Low)

Models emit structurally different PRE-FLIGHT header formats despite identical format specifications. Low severity for trust, but creates significant eval-engineering challenges.

### 5.7 Stochastic Instability (Medium)

Same model, same fixture, same prompt — produces different compliance outcomes across runs. Protocol compliance is a distribution, not a binary.

